Collections Framework:
-> Collections interfaces hierarchy: Collections, List, Queue, Dequeue, Map

The root interfaces are divided into two main branches:

Collection Interface (java.util.Collection) → represents a group of objects.
Subinterfaces: List, Set, Queue, Deque

Map Interface (java.util.Map) → represents key-value pairs.
Independent from Collection, but part of the framework.

1. Collection (root interface)

Superinterface for most collections (except Map).
Defines basic operations: add(), remove(), size(), iterator().

2. List (ordered collection, allows duplicates)

Maintains insertion order.
Allows duplicate elements.
Access elements by index.
Implementations:
ArrayList → resizable array, fast random access.
LinkedList → doubly linked list, fast insertion/deletion.
Vector → synchronized version (legacy).
Stack → subclass of Vector, LIFO stack.

3. Set (unordered collection, no duplicates)

Does not allow duplicate elements.
No index-based access.
Implementations:
HashSet → backed by hash table, no order.
LinkedHashSet → maintains insertion order.
TreeSet → sorted set (Red-Black tree).

4. Queue (FIFO ordering, except priority rules)

Used to hold elements before processing.
Typically FIFO (First In, First Out).
Implementations:
PriorityQueue → elements ordered by priority (natural order or comparator).
LinkedList → can also act as a queue.

5. Deque (Double-Ended Queue)

A subtype of Queue.
Allows insertion and removal from both ends.
Implementations:
ArrayDeque → resizable array, efficient stack & queue.
LinkedList → also implements Deque.

6. Map (key-value pairs, no duplicate keys)

Not a child of Collection.
Maps unique keys to values.
Implementations:
HashMap → stores entries in hash table (no order).
LinkedHashMap → maintains insertion order.
TreeMap → sorted by keys (Red-Black tree).
Hashtable → synchronized legacy version.

List Implementations:

-> ArrayList vs LinkedList (use cases, time complexities)

| Feature                   | **ArrayList**                                                                                      | **LinkedList**                                                                                         |
| ------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **Internal structure**    | Dynamic **array**                                                                                  | Doubly **linked list**                                                                                 |
| **Access (get by index)** | O(1) → fast random access                                                                          | O(n) → must traverse                                                                                   |
| **Insertion at end**      | Amortized O(1)                                                                                     | O(1)                                                                                                   |
| **Insertion in middle**   | O(n) (shifts elements)                                                                             | O(1) (if position known via iterator)                                                                  |
| **Deletion at end**       | O(1)                                                                                               | O(1)                                                                                                   |
| **Deletion in middle**    | O(n) (shift elements)                                                                              | O(1) (if node known)                                                                                   |
| **Memory usage**          | Compact (just elements array)                                                                      | Extra memory for pointers (prev/next)                                                                  |
| **Use cases**             | - Frequent **access by index** <br> - Read-heavy operations <br> - Example: lookup tables, caching | - Frequent **insertions/deletions** <br> - Write-heavy operations <br> - Example: queues, undo history |


Vector

Grows like an ArrayList but thread-safe (all methods synchronized).
Slower than ArrayList in single-threaded apps due to synchronization overhead.
Mostly replaced by ArrayList (unless synchronization is explicitly needed).

Stack

Extends Vector.
Implements LIFO (Last-In-First-Out) stack with push(), pop(), peek().
Considered legacy → better to use Deque (ArrayDeque) for modern stack implementations.

-> CopyOnWriteArrayList (thread-safe)

Part of java.util.concurrent.
A thread-safe version of ArrayList.
Works by making a new copy of the array whenever you modify it (add, set, remove).
Characteristics:
Read operations: O(1), very fast (no locks needed).
Write operations: O(n), since it copies the whole list.
Iterators are fail-safe (they iterate over a snapshot, no ConcurrentModificationException).
Use cases:
Best when:
Reads >> Writes (many reads, very few writes).
Example: maintaining a read-mostly configuration list or listeners in event systems.

